    </answer>
    <solution><p>Since <m>F(0)=0</m>, <m>F(b)=\int_0^{b} f(t)\,dt</m>.  For each <m>b</m> we
determine <m>F(b)</m> graphically: for regions where <m>f</m> is constant, the
integral over that region is just (length)(value of f); for regions that
are triangular or trapezoidal, we calculate the area using either of 
<m>\frac12</m>(base)(height) (where the height is given by the value of <m>f</m>
and may be negative), or (length)(average height).  Thus,</p>
<p><m>F(0)=0</m></p>
<p><m>F(1)= F(0)  - 1 = 0  - 1 = -1</m></p>
<p><m>F(2)= F(1) + 1 = -1 + 1 = -2</m></p>
<p><m>F(3)= F(2)  - 1 = -2  - 1 = -3</m></p>
<p><m>F(4)= F(3)  - 0.5 = -3  - 0.5 = -3.5</m></p>
<p><m>F(5)= F(4) + 0.5 = -3.5 + 0.5 = -3</m></p>
<p>and, finally,</p>
<p><m>F(6)= F(5) + 0.5 = -3 + 0.5 = -2.5</m>.</p>
</solution>
